Notes on the limit_traffic_rate module
=====

To install, compile nginx with this ./configure option:

    --add-module=path/to/this/directory

Nginx directive limit_rate could limit connection's speed, and limit_conn 
could limit connection number by given variable. If the client is a browser,
 it only open one connection to the server. The speed will be limited to 
limit_rate, unless the client is a multi-thread download tool.

Limit_traffic_rate module provides a method to limit the total download rate
 by client IP or download URL, even there are several connections. The 
limit condition could be defined by the following directive.

The limit_traffic_rate module need to use a share memory pool. Directive 
syntax is same to limit_zone. 

    http {
        #limit_traffic_rate_zone   rate $request_uri 32m;
        limit_traffic_rate_zone   rate $remote_addr 32m;
        
        server {
            location /download/ {
                limit_traffic_rate  rate 20k;
            }
        }
    }

If the core limit_rate directive, the $limit_rate variable or the
X-Accel-Limit-Rate header sets a lower rate for a request, the lower rate
is used.

How the total rate is enforced:

  * All bytes sent for a variable value are counted, including those of
    requests that have already finished, so the limit also applies to
    many short responses, whether they are sent in parallel or one after
    another.
  * The bandwidth is shared between the active requests. If more than
    rate * seconds bytes have been sent, requests are slowed down until
    the total is within the limit again.
  * At most 1 second of unused bandwidth is saved up, so a burst after an
    idle period is limited to about "rate" bytes (plus the data in flight).
  * The rate is measured in whole seconds and enforced through the nginx
    write filter, so the rate over a short interval can be higher; over
    several seconds it stays close to the limit.
  * After the last request for a value finishes, its state is kept while
    it is still needed (the bytes sent are over the limit, or less than 1
    second of bandwidth is unused). Such idle states are freed when they
    are no longer needed, or, if the zone is full, oldest first. So a zone
    that is too small makes the limit less accurate for idle clients.
  * The first limit_rate_after bytes of each response are neither limited
    nor counted.
    So responses smaller than limit_rate_after are never counted toward
    the total.

Changelogs
  v0.2 
    *   modify algorithm, rate = (limit - last_rate)/conn + last_rate
  v0.1
    *   first release

